# Proof files are bytes, not text.
#
# Rule R17 binds each probe to `evidence/probes/<id>.probe.log` by path AND sha256, and the
# whole value of that binding is that the NEXT machine can check it. With no rule here, this
# repository inherits `core.autocrlf` from whoever cloned it: on this workstation autocrlf=true,
# so git stored the eleven evidence files with LF and checked them out with CRLF, and the hashes
# I recorded were the hashes of the CRLF working-tree bytes. Clone the repo anywhere with
# autocrlf=false -- Linux CI, any Windows box set to `input`, most containers -- and the same
# eleven files arrive with LF, `registry-verify.py` exits 1, and R17 reports every retained run
# as "changed under the registry".
#
# That was measured, not imagined: an independent verifier cloned with
# `git -c core.autocrlf=false clone` and got 11 R17 violations, while the same clone on this
# box's inherited setting exited 0. A hash that depends on a checkout filter is not evidence of
# anything except the filter.
#
# `-text` means: store the bytes exactly as written, check them out exactly as stored, on every
# platform and under every autocrlf setting. It is the right call for these files specifically --
# they are OUTPUT, byte-compared by a hash, and a line-ending conversion would silently change
# the thing being compared.
evidence/probes/*.log -text

# 2026-11-01: THE SAME ARGUMENT APPLIES TO CONTENT REWRITES, NOT ONLY TO CHECKOUT FILTERS.
#
# The history rewrite that took the machine's identity strings out of this repository applied its
# substitution to EVERY blob, these files included, and replaced the real paths inside eleven
# captured runs with `%USERPROFILE%` / `<private-tree>`. Eleven of the twenty-five `probe.sha256`
# values stopped matching and R17 fired on all eleven.
#
# The bytes were restored from the pre-filter bundle and the registry was NOT edited. Recording a
# new hash over a rewritten transcript would make the registry attest a run that never happened --
# the log would say the probe printed `%USERPROFILE%` where it printed a real path -- which is the
# same defect as the checkout filter above, one layer up. Evidence is what the run emitted; a
# sanitised transcript is evidence of the sanitiser.
#
# So: ANY future content-rewrite pass -- `git filter-repo --replace-text`, a redaction script --
# MUST exclude `evidence/probes/`. Those files are frozen on purpose. The publish trade is stated
# rather than hidden: the evidence names the author's machine, and the alternatives are to ship it
# as-is or to drop/regenerate it. There is no third option that keeps it evidence. R17 is the
# detector if this is ever ignored again; it fired on all eleven, which is how this was found.

